# Sum of Positive

```javascript
function positiveSum(arr) {
  return arr.reduce((total, n) => (n > 0 ? total + n : total), 0);
}
```

A single left-to-right pass, adding each element only when it is positive. Seeding `reduce` with `0` also gives the right answer for an empty array and for an array with no positive values, both of which the notes call out.

O(n) time, O(1) extra space.

# Highest and Lowest

```javascript
function highAndLow(numbers){
  const values = numbers.split(' ').map(Number);
  return `${Math.max(...values)} ${Math.min(...values)}`;
}
```

Split on the single space the notes guarantee, convert to numbers, then take both extremes in one pass each. The result is a string of the highest and lowest separated by a space, which is what the sample tests assert.

O(n) time, O(n) space for the parsed values.

# Longest Run of Equal Values

> **Missing:** No language was stated in the transcript, so this assumes JavaScript.

```javascript
function longestRun(values) {
  let longest = 0;
  let current = 0;
  let previous;
  for (const value of values) {
    current = value === previous ? current + 1 : 1;
    longest = Math.max(longest, current);
    previous = value;
  }
  return longest;
}
```

One left-to-right pass, extending a running streak whenever the current value matches the one right before it and resetting the streak to one otherwise, tracking the longest streak seen so far. `[1, 1, 2, 2, 2, 3]` walks to a longest run of three, matching the example given out loud.

O(n) time, O(1) extra space.

# Balanced Brackets

> **Missing:** No language was stated in the transcript, so this assumes Python.

```python
def is_balanced(text):
    pairs = {')': '(', ']': '[', '}': '{'}
    stack = []
    for character in text:
        if character in '([{':
            stack.append(character)
        elif character in ')]}':
            if not stack or stack.pop() != pairs[character]:
                return False
    return not stack
```

A stack holding every open bracket seen so far: each close bracket has to match the most recently opened one, and popping it off confirms the nesting closes in the right order. Anything left on the stack at the end means something was opened and never closed. `[( )]` balances to an empty stack; `( ]` fails the moment `]` doesn't match the `(` still on top.

O(n) time, O(n) space for the stack.

